## Start with the result

![Terrain, surface height, vegetation p95, return density, terrain differences and vegetation-return fraction. Missing observations remain blank.](report/assets/products.png)

The example is mixed woodland/open space near Autzen Stadium, Oregon. It demonstrates the method; it does not establish forest inventory accuracy, fuel load or fire risk.

## A workflow worth inspecting

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  A["Pinned LAS/LAZ"] --> B["Inspect CRS + units"]
  B --> C["Transform + buffer + filter"]
  C --> D["Supplied ground"]
  C --> E["SMRF ground"]
  D --> F["Terrain + vegetation metrics"]
  E --> G["Alternative terrain"]
  F --> H["Compare + quality audit"]
  G --> H
```

- **[Read the workflow](report/methods.qmd):** separate horizontal/vertical feet, preserve source labels and document interpolation.
- **[Inspect quality findings](report/quality.qmd):** missing support, negative heights and ground-label disagreement.
- **[Reproduce the analysis](report/reproduce.qmd):** CLI, notebook, configuration and checks.

## Technical evidence

**laspy, PDAL, pyproj, SciPy and Rasterio** produce GeoTIFFs, a normalized LAZ with extra dimensions, height profiles, figures, runtime metadata and hashes. Processing is scripted; the notebook explains and inspects results.

No API key. The report renders from bundled figures and manifest. Running the analysis downloads approximately 74 MB; point data stays outside Git.

